# Docs terminology — control plane ≠ framework

## Definitions (from [docs/glossary.md](../../docs/glossary.md))

| Term | Means | Does **not** mean |
|------|--------|-------------------|
| **Control plane** | Configure-and-gate: Control Plane dashboard, opt-in/opt-out, audit, prelude verification | The whole product, distribution, lock compilation, schedule clients, or agent runtime |
| **Control Plane dashboard** | The per-repo dashboard issue with enable checkboxes | A synonym for “control plane” as a whole, or for the framework |
| **Framework** / **oblt-aw** | Routes, client templates, distribution, catalog, and the control plane as one product | Use “control plane” when you only mean enablement/gating |

## Required wording

- Enable / disable / checkbox / prelude gate → **control plane** or **Control Plane dashboard**.
- Locks, AW compilation, schedule lanes/clients, routes, distribution, capability wiring, agent runtime → **framework** or **oblt-aw**.
- When both matter in one sentence, name them separately (framework owns X; control plane gates Y).

## Forbidden patterns

- Saying the control plane “owns” or “runs” runtime, locks, compilation, schedule clients, or distribution.
- Using “control plane” as a casual synonym for the `elastic/oblt-aw` product or repository role when you mean the framework.

